      "title": "Interim TBE vaccination guidance",
      "publicationDate": "2026-03-02",
      "effectiveDate": "2026-03-02",
      "issuingAuthority": "USEUCOM Command Surgeon, CAPT Wayne R. Smith",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.eucom.mil/staff-resources/eucom-theatre-medical-clearance-information",
      "retrievedAt": "2026-10-11",
      "access": "user_supplied_ocr",
      "uploadedFilename": "PDFgear_OCR_EUCOM.txt",
      "startLine": 1,
      "endLine": 38,
      "sourceText": "UNCLASSIFIED\nUNITED STATES EUROPEAN COMMAND\nUNIT 30400\nAPO AE 09154\n2 MAR 2026\nMEMORANDUM FOR DISTRIBUTION\nSUBJECT:\nInterim Guidance on Tick-Borne Encephalitis (TBE) Vaccination\nReferences:\n(a) ECI 4202.01A, Theater Medical Entry Requirements, 24 July 2025\n(b) Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Tick-Borne Encephalitis (TBE)\nClinical and Travel Guidance.\n(c) European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC). Tick-Borne\nEncephalitis Surveillance and Epidemiology.\n(d) U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). TICOVAC™M TBE Vaccine Approval\nLetter. August 13, 2021.\n1. This memorandum provides interim guidance and clarification regarding the Tick-Borne Encephalitis\n(TBE) vaccine policy outlined in reference (a) for the USEUCOM Area of Responsibility. TBE is a\npotentially chronic and deadly disease present across many parts of Europe, particularly northern, central\nand eastern Europe. High-risk areas for TBE are defined as countries with >1/100,000 confirmed TBE on\nthe most recent annual epidemiological report, available here: https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/tick-borne-\n2. Effective immediately, the language in ECI 4202.01A, (Enclosure C, p. C-5, para. 5.b.(12)), stating\nthat the Tick borne encephalitis (TBE) vaccine (Tico Vac™M) is \"required\" is superseded by this guidance.\nPer references (b) thru (d), the Tick-borne encephalitis (TBE) vaccine is \"highly recommended\" for all\nDepartment of War (DoW) personnel, (AD, Reservists on AD orders, DoW civilians and/or contractors in\nsupport of military operations), travelling to high-risk areas for TBE in Europe who will be working or\nspending time in rural, forested, or brushy areas. For personnel working or spending time in urban\nsettings within high-risk areas, the TBE vaccine is generally not necessary, as the risk of exposure to\ninfected ticks in these settings is considered low.\n3. This change will be formally incorporated into the next annual review and update of ECI 4204.01A, at\nwhich time this interim guidance will be rescinded.\n4. The USEUCOM point of contact is: Lt Col Karen A. Vandor, US Air Force, Force Health Protection\nBranch Chief, at email: karen.a.vandor.mil@mail.mil, DSN: 324-412-4197.\nunfaith\nWAYNE R. SMITH\nCaptain, U.S. Navy\nCommand Surgeon\nUNCLASSIFIED",
